## Why This Is Asked

This question tests ability to design runtime-extensible pluggable components using a registry and strategy-like dispatch, with non-blocking rendering and graceful fallback in a UI/charting context.

## Key Concepts

- Registry pattern
- Strategy pattern
- Async/non-blocking execution
- Runtime extensibility

## Code Example

```typescript
interface Renderer { render(data: any, container: HTMLElement): Promise<void>; }
class RendererRegistry {
  private map = new Map<string, new () => Renderer>();
  register(name: string, ctor: new () => Renderer) { this.map.set(name, ctor); }
  get(name: string): Renderer { const ctor = this.map.get(name); return new (ctor!)(); }
}
class Chart {
  constructor(private registry: RendererRegistry) {}
  async render(data: any, container: HTMLElement, preferred?: string) {
    const renderer = this.registry.get(preferred ?? 'svg');
    await renderer.render(data, container);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement graceful fallback if the chosen renderer crashes mid-render?
- How would you expose adding/removing renderers at runtime without hot-reload?

