- How would you test per-channel success/failure, isolation, and retries?
- How would you extend to dynamically hot-swap channel implementations in production?

## Why This Is Asked
This question explores runtime extensibility and undoable actions through a practical pattern common in tooling and scripting domains.

## Key Concepts
- **Command pattern** with undo support
- **Runtime plugin registry** for lazy loading
- **Safe macro/sequence composition** with transactional semantics
- **Isolation and error handling** for plugins

## Code Example
```typescript
// types.ts
export interface Context {
  args: string[]
  env: Record<string, string>
}

export interface Command {
  name: string
  execute(ctx: Context): Promise<void>
  undo(ctx: Context): Promise<void>
}

export type CommandFactory = (name: string) => Command | Promise<Command>

// registry.ts
export class Registry {
  private factories = new Map<string, CommandFactory>()
  private commands = new Map<string, Command>()

  register(name: string, factory: CommandFactory): void {
    this.factories.set(name, factory)
  }

  async get(name: string): Promise<Command> {
    if (!this.commands.has(name)) {
      const factory = this.factories.get(name)
      if (!factory) throw new Error(`Command "${name}" not found`)
      this.commands.set(name, await factory(name))
    }
    return this.commands.get(name)!
  }
}

// macro.ts
export class Macro implements Command {
  name: string
  private commands: Command[]

  constructor(name: string, commands: Command[]) {
    this.name = name
    this.commands = commands
  }

  async execute(ctx: Context): Promise<void> {
    const executed: Command[] = []
    try {
      for (const cmd of this.commands) {
        await cmd.execute(ctx)
        executed.push(cmd)
      }
    } catch (err) {
      for (const cmd of executed.reverse()) {
        await cmd.undo(ctx)
      }
      throw err
    }
  }

  async undo(ctx: Context): Promise<void> {
    for (const cmd of this.commands.reverse()) {
      await cmd.undo(ctx)
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a runtime-extensible plugin system with concurrency considerations and clear trade-offs between design patterns.

## Key Concepts

- Strategy Pattern
- Registry/Service Locator
- Atomic/lock-free swaps for runtime configuration
- Deterministic ordering and starvation considerations
- Per-queue isolation in a multi-worker setting

## Code Example

```javascript
interface Strategy {
  dispatch(queueId: string, tasks: any[]): any[]
}

class Registry<T> {
  private map = new Map<string, T>()
  register(name: string, value: T) { this.map.set(name, value) }
  get(name: string): T | undefined { return this.map.get(name) }
}

class QueueEngine {
  private currentStrategy: Strategy;
  constructor(private registry: Registry<Strategy>, initial: string) {
    const s = registry.get(initial); if (!s) throw new Error('unknown strategy')
    this.currentStrategy = s
  }
  swapStrategy(name: string) {
    const s = this.registry.get(name); if (s) this.currentStrategy = s
  }
  dispatch(queueId: string, tasks: any[]) {
    return this.currentStrategy.dispatch(queueId, tasks)
  }
}

// Usage
class RoundRobin implements Strategy { dispatch(q: string, t: any[]) { return t } }
class WorkStealing implements Strategy { dispatch(q: string, t: any[]) { return t } }
class PriorityBased implements Strategy { dispatch(q: string, t: any[]) { return t } }

const reg = new Registry<Strategy>()
reg.register('rr', new RoundRobin())
reg.register('ws', new WorkStealing())
reg.register('pb', new PriorityBased())

const engine = new QueueEngine(reg, 'rr')
engine.swapStrategy('pb')
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test swapping under peak load?
- How to ensure per-queue isolation when multiple queues swap strategies concurrently?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses the ability to apply the Command pattern with a runtime registry for runtime extensibility, decoupling command invocation from implementation.

## Key Concepts
- Command pattern for encapsulating each request as an object
- Runtime extensibility through dynamic registration
- Isolation and error handling for fault tolerance
- Async execution for non-blocking operations

## Code Example
```typescript
interface Command {
  name: string;
  execute(args: unknown): Promise<void>;
}

type CommandFactory = () => Command;

class CommandRegistry {
  private map = new Map<string, CommandFactory>();

  register(name: string, factory: CommandFactory): void {
    this.map.set(name, factory);
  }

  async execute(name: string, args: unknown): Promise<void> {
    const factory = this.map.get(name);
    if (!factory) throw new Error(`Unknown command: ${name}`);
    const command = factory();
    try {
      await command.execute(args);
    } catch (err) {
      console.error(`Command '${name}' failed:`, err);
      process.exit(1);
    }
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new CommandRegistry();
registry.register('init', () => new InitCommand());
registry.register('build', () => new BuildCommand());
registry.register('deploy', () => new DeployCommand());
await registry.execute('build', { target: 'prod' });
```

## Design Pattern
The **Command pattern** is ideal here because it encapsulates each operation as an executable object, enabling delayed execution, queuing, and undo functionality if needed.

## Trade-offs
- **Pros**: Loose coupling, runtime extensibility, easy testing (mock commands)
- **Cons**: Additional indirection, slightly more complex codebase, registry can become a global state hotspot

